      "revision": 106369229,
      "text": "ナギナタコウジュ は、シソ科ナギナタコウジュ属に分類される一年生草本の植物。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Elsholtzia ciliata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elsholtzia_ciliata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1331588575,
      "text": "Elsholtzia ciliata, commonly known as Vietnamese balm, comb mint, xiang ru (香薷) or kinh giới in Vietnamese, is a flowering plant in the family Lamiaceae native to Asia. In the US, it is commonly known as crested late summer mint. In US Vietnamese grocery stores, it is called kinh gioi, Vietnamese lemon balm, or Vietnamese lemon mint."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Elsholtzia ciliata」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elsholtzia_ciliata#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1331588575,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "It is cultivated as an ornamental plant. It prefers moist soil, and grows mostly on exposed rocky slopes and other open, gravelly areas.\nIt is banned in the state of Connecticut and is classified as a noxious weed. It was first reported in the Americas in 1889.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
